// so i think it better to change the way we store the types in general so first
// blocks
// block and machine will only take a return type and if it don't have one will be take all (block or machine) scopes
// so name<block> // will take any block regard of the return type same with machine
// to set a return type name<block<<return type>>> ex : name<block<int32>>
// micro and method and lambda and fn 
// will be name<<block type><(<params types>),<return type>>> ex :  name<fn<(int32,int32),int32>>
// but when it be name<<block type>>
// with no set <(<params types>),<return type>> it will take a block typed scope regard of other types (par ty,ret ty)
// and for name<<block type>><(<params types>)> it will take a block typed (with the set params) scope regard of other types (ret ty)
// and for name<<block type>><<return type>> it will take a block typed (with the set return) scope regard of other types (par ty)
// that is all it
// Object 
// so we need to add a new way to so
// so i think we will allow two write the name of the Object  def after a ::
// ex name<class::X> x = new X();
// that will allow us to override Object Def
// and i think that will be enough setting for Objects and their Defs
// also i think the ability to set a certain def need it in Blocks
// first what we have the ability to override is fns and methods and micro and lambda 
// no other think are allowed for now at least
// so we allow the ability to override them because they can take a deferent <(<params types>),<return type>>
// also i think we can allow the ability to override the type so fn version and method version but they must be have deferent <(<params types>),<return type>>
// i think we can even allow the ability to compactly override the type so even with the same <(<params types>),<return type>>
// and to call it you will select the type first that of course will happen if there if a two func with the same  <(<params types>),<return type>> and name
// so in this specific case if you write this <name>(); and there is two func with the same name but deferent type
// that will throw an error that say "there is more than one of <name> with deferent type so to \n use it you need first to set the type : <type>::<name>();"
// so to use a overridden func has more one implementation in deferent types
// you will do this : <type>::<name>();
// that give us the ability to know what we need to i think also maybe 
// if we can set a selected fn or what ever 
// so if we have some thing like that
// name<fn::double> double_ref; // that will be a ref for all the fn double version
// so: double_ref(10);// will work
// and : double_ref(10.0);// will work also
// we also will allow to set the <(<params types>),<return type>> by add them after the name
// like this name<fn::double<(float32)>>
// why we need name<<type>::<name><(<params types>),<return type>>>
// first i made that to fix modify<T,micro>
// so  modify<T,micro> is one of two expected for for in next handle
// but what will happen if T == micro 
// that will destroy every think so now it will be like that 
// modify<T,micro::stop<(()),()>> or modify<T,micro::stop<(void),void>> or modify<T,micro::stop<(),>>
// so we will make when we add () or leave the params empty () mean void
// to make it easy to explain
// adding <(),> after the type or the name will take only the  func(method, micro, fn , lambda) that don't have  params and a return
// why we need that because when we only say the type or the name that will make it take all version of that name or any func fom that type
// also <(()),()> witch mean <(void),void> will work the same way
impl array {
    fn iterator() -> void {
        this.count := 0;
    }
      fn next() -> modify<T,micro::stop<(),>> { //now it will only take the expected micro stop
        if(this.count >= this.length) return stop; // i think the con is wrong but you get it
         this.count += 1;
          return array[this.count - 1];
      }
}
