// ==============================================================================
// Access Handles Architecture (property_access, namespace_access, handle_access, arrow ->, fat_arrow =>)
// ==============================================================================
// For a handle to be recognized as an access handle, it must:
// 1. Take a single argument of type `MemberType`.
// 2. Return a `raw_ptr<unknown>`.
//
// Types & Semantics:
// - `MemberType`: Contains the member name as `char[]` and expected type as `type`.
// - `unknown`: A compile-time placeholder type that the compiler MUST deduce and narrow.
//   It is replaced at compile time with the concrete type of the accessed field.
// - Read vs Write: Returning `raw_ptr<unknown>` handles both read and write seamlessly:
//   * Read (`val = obj.field`): Implicit dereference of the returned pointer.
//   * Write (`obj.field = val`): Implicit write to the memory address of the returned pointer.
//
// Resolution Priorities:
// 1. Direct Parent Elements: Blueprint fields and methods have top priority (direct offset, zero overhead).
// 2. Overload Fallbacks: If an element is not directly present on the parent, the compiler checks:
//    `property_access`, `namespace_access`, `handle_access`.
// 3. Arrow Operators (`->`, `=>`): Any handle taking `MemberType` and returning `raw_ptr<unknown>`
//    is registered as an explicit access handle.
//
// Compiler Intrinsic (@compile::access):
// - In `std.fast` compile declaration block:
//   `set access<T>(T, MemberType) -> raw_ptr<unknown>;`
// - Evaluated strictly at compile time.
// - Throws a compile-time error immediately if the member does not exist on the target object.

blueprint MemberType = {
    char name[];
    type ty;
}

blueprint x = {
    int32 y;
}

impl x {
    handle -> {
        fn property_access(prop: MemberType) -> raw_ptr<unknown> {
            return @compile::access(this.y, prop);
        }
    }
}
